1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,960
This is Jensen, and Jensen has a huge problem.

2
00:00:03,960 --> 00:00:06,680
For 33 years, he built one company.

3
00:00:06,680 --> 00:00:08,960
It almost went broke, and he saved it.

4
00:00:08,960 --> 00:00:11,480
He bet a huge share of his profits on software

5
00:00:11,480 --> 00:00:14,600
nobody wanted and waited 10 years to be right.

6
00:00:14,600 --> 00:00:16,320
He turned a video game chip maker

7
00:00:16,320 --> 00:00:18,880
into the most valuable company on earth.

8
00:00:18,880 --> 00:00:22,160
33 years, and this week he lost it

9
00:00:22,160 --> 00:00:24,400
because he couldn't keep his pants zipped.

10
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:26,120
We accept your resignation.

11
00:00:26,120 --> 00:00:27,480
Can I keep the jacket?

12
00:00:27,480 --> 00:00:28,320
No.

13
00:00:28,320 --> 00:00:30,040
But that's Jensen's problem.

14
00:00:30,040 --> 00:00:31,360
Here's Nvidia's.

15
00:00:31,360 --> 00:00:33,440
Nobody thought he would actually go.

16
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:37,360
Two days before he quit, the betting markets gave it 18%

17
00:00:37,360 --> 00:00:40,040
because nobody could picture Nvidia without him.

18
00:00:40,040 --> 00:00:42,080
Nvidia never had a number two.

19
00:00:42,080 --> 00:00:44,960
60 people reported straight to one man.

20
00:00:44,960 --> 00:00:46,720
No backup, no plan B.

21
00:00:46,720 --> 00:00:48,760
There has to be somebody else.

22
00:00:48,760 --> 00:00:50,200
The guy in the leather jacket.

23
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:51,160
That's Jensen.

24
00:00:51,160 --> 00:00:53,840
The other guy in the leather jacket?

25
00:00:53,840 --> 00:00:56,040
Wall Street has even put a price on it.

26
00:00:56,040 --> 00:00:59,280
One analyst says Nvidia is worth $300 a share

27
00:00:59,280 --> 00:01:02,840
with Jensen in that jacket, and $225 with it empty.

28
00:01:02,840 --> 00:01:05,680
That gap is almost $2 trillion.

29
00:01:05,680 --> 00:01:08,280
Right now, the finance chief is holding it.

30
00:01:08,280 --> 00:01:10,440
The president wants to hand it to Tim Cook,

31
00:01:10,440 --> 00:01:12,760
and on Tuesday, Nvidia reports its numbers

32
00:01:12,760 --> 00:01:14,840
without him for the first time ever.

33
00:01:14,840 --> 00:01:18,280
So how does a man throw away 33 years of work?

34
00:01:18,280 --> 00:01:19,760
We've told that story already.

35
00:01:19,760 --> 00:01:22,240
The big question is the one he left behind.

36
00:01:22,240 --> 00:01:24,320
Can Nvidia survive without him?

37
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:30,040
April 1993, Jensen Huang and two other engineers

38
00:01:32,680 --> 00:01:34,400
start a graphics chip company.

39
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:35,720
They call it Nvidia.

40
00:01:35,720 --> 00:01:39,600
Jensen, Microsoft just set the rules for PC games.

41
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:41,280
Everything's triangles.

42
00:01:41,280 --> 00:01:43,760
Our chip does curves.

43
00:01:43,760 --> 00:01:45,040
Only curves.

44
00:01:45,040 --> 00:01:47,320
Nvidia has cut a beautiful key

45
00:01:47,320 --> 00:01:49,400
for a lock that just got changed.

46
00:01:49,400 --> 00:01:51,960
Worse, its biggest customer, Sega,

47
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:55,320
is paying for the next chip built the same wrong way.

48
00:01:55,320 --> 00:01:57,560
So Jensen flies to Japan.

49
00:01:57,560 --> 00:01:59,040
Our chip is a dead end.

50
00:01:59,040 --> 00:02:00,280
Cancel our contract.

51
00:02:00,280 --> 00:02:01,560
Hire someone else.

52
00:02:01,560 --> 00:02:03,800
You flew here to fire yourself?

53
00:02:03,800 --> 00:02:06,320
Yes, and keep the money you still owe us

54
00:02:06,320 --> 00:02:08,320
inside Nvidia as an investment.

55
00:02:08,320 --> 00:02:10,560
In the company whose chip doesn't work.

56
00:02:10,560 --> 00:02:11,960
You'll probably lose it.

57
00:02:11,960 --> 00:02:13,480
Sega said yes.

58
00:02:13,480 --> 00:02:16,920
$5 million, and it kept Nvidia alive.

59
00:02:16,920 --> 00:02:17,760
The reason?

60
00:02:17,760 --> 00:02:20,240
Sega's boss simply believed in him.

61
00:02:20,240 --> 00:02:21,680
How long have we got?

62
00:02:22,520 --> 00:02:23,520
Same as always.

63
00:02:23,520 --> 00:02:25,080
That was the company's motto.

64
00:02:25,080 --> 00:02:27,320
30 days from going out of business.

65
00:02:27,320 --> 00:02:32,000
In 1997, its next chip sold about a million in four months.

66
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:33,240
And within a few years,

67
00:02:33,240 --> 00:02:35,760
Nvidia was the king of gaming chips.

68
00:02:35,760 --> 00:02:39,760
Then in 2006, Jensen spent a big piece of the money

69
00:02:39,760 --> 00:02:41,920
on something gamers would never see.

70
00:02:41,920 --> 00:02:43,120
He called it CUDA.

71
00:02:43,120 --> 00:02:46,240
A graphics chip does thousands of tiny sums at once.

72
00:02:46,240 --> 00:02:48,520
Scientists could use that for anything.

73
00:02:48,520 --> 00:02:49,640
Who pays for it?

74
00:02:49,640 --> 00:02:51,040
Nobody, yet.

75
00:02:51,040 --> 00:02:54,080
It cost Nvidia a huge share of its profits.

76
00:02:54,080 --> 00:02:56,720
And for years, it earned almost nothing.

77
00:02:56,720 --> 00:02:59,080
Three years, still flat.

78
00:02:59,080 --> 00:03:00,680
It's eating our margins.

79
00:03:00,680 --> 00:03:01,520
It's early.

80
00:03:01,520 --> 00:03:04,200
I can't put early in a forecast.

81
00:03:04,200 --> 00:03:05,920
Then leave it out of the forecast.

82
00:03:05,920 --> 00:03:06,920
Keep it in the company.

83
00:03:06,920 --> 00:03:11,080
In 2012, a team in Toronto won an image recognition contest

84
00:03:11,080 --> 00:03:13,120
by a mile, with a program trained

85
00:03:13,120 --> 00:03:15,320
on two Nvidia gaming cards.

86
00:03:15,320 --> 00:03:17,800
Every AI lab on Earth noticed.

87
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:19,880
CUDA took a decade to pay off.

88
00:03:19,920 --> 00:03:22,560
Today, nine of every 10 graphics chips

89
00:03:22,560 --> 00:03:24,560
that run AI are Nvidia's.

90
00:03:24,560 --> 00:03:25,840
And that finance chief?

91
00:03:25,840 --> 00:03:26,640
He was right.

92
00:03:26,640 --> 00:03:28,520
Every year, CUDA lost money.

93
00:03:28,520 --> 00:03:30,440
Cutting it was the sensible call.

94
00:03:30,440 --> 00:03:33,240
Even the investors who think Nvidia will be fine

95
00:03:33,240 --> 00:03:35,720
give Jensen's pipeline two years.

96
00:03:35,720 --> 00:03:37,600
So who signs year three?

97
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:38,560
As of this week,

98
00:03:38,560 --> 00:03:41,120
Nvidia's boss is its finance chief.

99
00:03:43,360 --> 00:03:44,560
Back to that test.

100
00:03:44,560 --> 00:03:48,160
Name one Nvidia executive who isn't Jensen Huang.

101
00:03:48,160 --> 00:03:49,120
You can't.

102
00:03:49,120 --> 00:03:50,360
That was on purpose.

103
00:03:50,360 --> 00:03:55,040
For years, Jensen was Nvidia's only face, inside and out.

104
00:03:55,040 --> 00:03:57,920
Most big companies are built like an office tower.

105
00:03:57,920 --> 00:04:00,280
Nvidia was built like a bicycle wheel.

106
00:04:00,280 --> 00:04:03,000
60 people reported straight to Jensen.

107
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:06,040
No chief operating officer, no one-on-ones.

108
00:04:06,040 --> 00:04:09,200
I don't have one-on-ones with them because it's impossible.

109
00:04:09,200 --> 00:04:11,840
He even reviewed every employee's pay.

110
00:04:11,840 --> 00:04:13,080
Who needs a decision?

111
00:04:13,080 --> 00:04:13,920
Me.

112
00:04:13,920 --> 00:04:16,280
Yes, yes, no, yes, next.

113
00:04:16,280 --> 00:04:18,160
It made Nvidia fast.

114
00:04:18,200 --> 00:04:19,640
In Jensen's own words,

115
00:04:19,640 --> 00:04:22,120
my management style is what made it possible

116
00:04:22,120 --> 00:04:23,440
for the company to grow.

117
00:04:23,440 --> 00:04:25,000
It also meant this.

118
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:27,000
So who's number two?

119
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:27,920
There isn't one.

120
00:04:27,920 --> 00:04:29,680
No succession plan, either.

121
00:04:29,680 --> 00:04:31,560
So what was the hub holding?

122
00:04:31,560 --> 00:04:33,000
First, the customers.

123
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:34,840
Who buys all these chips?

124
00:04:34,840 --> 00:04:36,320
Three direct buyers.

125
00:04:36,320 --> 00:04:39,640
About 44 cents of every dollar this year so far.

126
00:04:39,640 --> 00:04:41,040
And who did they deal with?

127
00:04:41,040 --> 00:04:41,880
Guess.

128
00:04:41,880 --> 00:04:44,560
One investor, Thomas Ferguson, said this.

129
00:04:44,560 --> 00:04:46,600
As a student of economic history,

130
00:04:46,600 --> 00:04:49,240
I don't know if there's ever been such a key man

131
00:04:49,240 --> 00:04:51,600
holding so much of this industry up.

132
00:04:51,600 --> 00:04:54,080
And that industry includes your money.

133
00:04:54,080 --> 00:04:55,840
I don't own Nvidia.

134
00:04:55,840 --> 00:04:58,440
Got an S&P 500 fund in there?

135
00:04:58,440 --> 00:04:59,280
Yes.

136
00:04:59,280 --> 00:05:00,720
Then you own Nvidia.

137
00:05:00,720 --> 00:05:04,040
Put $10,000 in an S&P 500 fund

138
00:05:04,040 --> 00:05:06,640
and six or 700 of it is Nvidia.

139
00:05:06,640 --> 00:05:09,320
One of the biggest bets in your whole retirement

140
00:05:09,320 --> 00:05:11,240
is riding on one company.

141
00:05:11,240 --> 00:05:13,880
And that company was riding on one man.

142
00:05:13,880 --> 00:05:15,680
I never picked this guy.

143
00:05:15,680 --> 00:05:16,840
You didn't have to.

144
00:05:16,840 --> 00:05:19,280
You just had to save for retirement.

145
00:05:19,280 --> 00:05:22,080
So my retirement rides on Tuesday

146
00:05:22,080 --> 00:05:23,760
and I didn't even get a vote.

147
00:05:23,760 --> 00:05:25,920
Everybody has a call on Tuesday.

148
00:05:25,920 --> 00:05:27,760
Most people never act on it.

149
00:05:27,760 --> 00:05:30,720
Nvidia sits inside a fund they never picked.

150
00:05:30,720 --> 00:05:32,440
I do have a theory.

151
00:05:32,440 --> 00:05:34,840
I'm just not betting my retirement on it.

152
00:05:34,840 --> 00:05:35,840
You don't have to.

