173
00:06:33,920 --> 00:06:35,560
Now, back to Nvidia.

174
00:06:35,560 --> 00:06:39,360
For 33 years, one man made the big bets there.

175
00:06:39,400 --> 00:06:42,560
As of this week, someone very different makes them.

176
00:06:45,160 --> 00:06:46,960
Thursday, November 12th,

177
00:06:46,960 --> 00:06:49,480
Nvidia's new boss is Colette Kress,

178
00:06:49,480 --> 00:06:51,840
13 years running Nvidia's money.

179
00:06:51,840 --> 00:06:55,480
Before that, Cisco, Microsoft and Texas Instruments.

180
00:06:55,480 --> 00:06:58,040
Her job in public was reading out the results.

181
00:06:58,040 --> 00:07:01,400
Now she's interim CEO and still CFO

182
00:07:01,400 --> 00:07:03,920
so the finance chief reports to herself.

183
00:07:03,920 --> 00:07:06,880
I'd like money for something bold.

184
00:07:06,880 --> 00:07:08,160
Show me the numbers.

185
00:07:08,200 --> 00:07:09,680
There aren't any yet.

186
00:07:09,680 --> 00:07:11,480
Then come back when there are.

187
00:07:11,480 --> 00:07:12,920
Is she the new Jensen?

188
00:07:12,920 --> 00:07:16,480
She's the one to steady the ship for a year.

189
00:07:16,480 --> 00:07:18,320
Then they hire an outsider.

190
00:07:18,320 --> 00:07:20,320
That's what an interim boss is for.

191
00:07:20,320 --> 00:07:23,840
Keep customers calm, hit the numbers, break nothing.

192
00:07:23,840 --> 00:07:26,040
We've seen this exact move before.

193
00:07:26,040 --> 00:07:29,520
August 2010, HP's CEO, Mark Herd,

194
00:07:29,520 --> 00:07:31,920
resigns after an investigation finds

195
00:07:31,920 --> 00:07:35,040
a close personal relationship with a contractor

196
00:07:35,040 --> 00:07:37,040
and inaccurate expense reports.

197
00:07:37,040 --> 00:07:39,320
So who runs HP now?

198
00:07:39,320 --> 00:07:40,840
Me, temporarily.

199
00:07:40,840 --> 00:07:45,040
In two weeks, HP was $15 billion lighter.

200
00:07:45,040 --> 00:07:49,080
One study later looked at six big CEO scandal exits.

201
00:07:49,080 --> 00:07:52,520
Over the next year, HP did the worst of all six,

202
00:07:52,520 --> 00:07:54,880
nearly 40 points behind the market.

203
00:07:54,880 --> 00:07:56,160
Intel tried it too.

204
00:07:56,160 --> 00:08:00,360
In 2018, its CEO resigned over a past relationship

205
00:08:00,360 --> 00:08:01,280
with an employee.

206
00:08:01,280 --> 00:08:03,400
So who runs Intel now?

207
00:08:03,400 --> 00:08:04,880
Me, temporarily.

208
00:08:04,880 --> 00:08:06,640
Want to make it permanent?

209
00:08:07,400 --> 00:08:11,440
Under Swan, Intel's next manufacturing step slipped again.

210
00:08:11,440 --> 00:08:15,240
And in 2021, it replaced him with an engineer too late.

211
00:08:15,240 --> 00:08:17,560
Last year, the company that had passed it,

212
00:08:17,560 --> 00:08:21,120
NVIDIA, put $5 billion into Intel.

213
00:08:21,120 --> 00:08:23,400
A burger chain can run on the spreadsheet.

214
00:08:23,400 --> 00:08:26,040
A kitchen with three Michelin stars can't.

215
00:08:26,040 --> 00:08:29,400
Its best dish exists because the chef tried something weird

216
00:08:29,400 --> 00:08:32,160
and said, again, NVIDIA is that kitchen.

217
00:08:32,160 --> 00:08:33,840
And the chef just walked out.

218
00:08:33,840 --> 00:08:35,480
Rivals can smell it.

219
00:08:35,520 --> 00:08:37,720
The first morning after the scandal broke,

220
00:08:37,720 --> 00:08:41,000
Google unveiled a new AI chip built for governments

221
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:42,600
and regulated industries.

222
00:08:42,600 --> 00:08:45,760
And every month NVIDIA goes without a permanent boss,

223
00:08:45,760 --> 00:08:47,960
rivals sales pitch gets easier.

224
00:08:47,960 --> 00:08:50,080
Steadying the ship is the job.

225
00:08:50,080 --> 00:08:51,800
Look at what calm costs.

226
00:08:51,800 --> 00:08:54,720
Colette, would you bet a huge share of our profits

227
00:08:54,720 --> 00:08:57,760
on something with no customers for years?

228
00:08:57,760 --> 00:08:58,840
What's the return?

229
00:08:58,840 --> 00:09:00,280
Nobody knows.

230
00:09:00,280 --> 00:09:01,680
Then no.

231
00:09:01,680 --> 00:09:02,880
Perfect answer.

232
00:09:02,880 --> 00:09:03,640
It is.

233
00:09:03,640 --> 00:09:06,280
It's also the answer that would have killed CUDA.

234
00:09:08,560 --> 00:09:10,800
Apple lost Steve Jobs twice.

235
00:09:10,800 --> 00:09:13,080
The first time, it nearly went broke.

236
00:09:13,080 --> 00:09:15,960
The second time, it became one of the richest companies

237
00:09:15,960 --> 00:09:16,800
on earth.

238
00:09:16,800 --> 00:09:19,280
The difference was who picked the next boss.

239
00:09:19,280 --> 00:09:23,360
1985, Apple's board strips Jobs of his duties.

240
00:09:23,360 --> 00:09:25,120
We're backing the professional, Steve.

241
00:09:25,120 --> 00:09:26,160
We'll take it from here.

242
00:09:26,160 --> 00:09:27,080
Sure you will.

243
00:09:27,080 --> 00:09:28,960
In September, he resigns.

244
00:09:28,960 --> 00:09:32,360
Apple goes through three CEOs in 12 years

245
00:09:32,360 --> 00:09:34,960
and a pile of forgettable computers.

246
00:09:34,960 --> 00:09:38,560
In two years, it loses almost $2 billion.

247
00:09:38,560 --> 00:09:42,680
When Jobs comes back, he takes $150 million from Bill Gates.

248
00:09:42,680 --> 00:09:44,920
Apple got rescued by Microsoft?

249
00:09:44,920 --> 00:09:46,240
That's how bad it got.

250
00:09:46,240 --> 00:09:48,120
2011 is the opposite.

251
00:09:48,120 --> 00:09:49,360
Jobs is dying.

252
00:09:49,360 --> 00:09:52,080
And he picks his successor himself, Tim Cook,

253
00:09:52,080 --> 00:09:55,080
who had run Apple's operations for years.

254
00:09:55,080 --> 00:09:56,000
It's yours, Tim.

255
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:57,600
You already know where everything is.

256
00:09:57,600 --> 00:10:01,680
Cook took over at about $347 billion.

257
00:10:01,680 --> 00:10:05,440
This September, Apple was worth $4.7 trillion.

258
00:10:05,440 --> 00:10:08,600
Nvidia, this week, is 1985.

259
00:10:08,600 --> 00:10:10,080
There was no plan B.

260
00:10:10,080 --> 00:10:13,000
In March, on a podcast, Jensen waved off

261
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:14,600
classic succession plans.

262
00:10:14,600 --> 00:10:16,800
His idea was to pass on what he knew

263
00:10:16,800 --> 00:10:19,360
as often and continuously as you can.

264
00:10:19,360 --> 00:10:22,800
And for 33 years, a company now worth trillions

265
00:10:22,800 --> 00:10:24,240
had no board chair.

266
00:10:24,240 --> 00:10:26,440
It announced its first one on Thursday.

267
00:10:26,440 --> 00:10:28,000
We have a chair now.

268
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:29,040
Since when?

269
00:10:29,040 --> 00:10:30,600
Since Wednesday night.

270
00:10:30,640 --> 00:10:32,120
So who's on the list?

271
00:10:32,120 --> 00:10:34,800
Colette, a few of Jensen's engineers.

272
00:10:34,800 --> 00:10:36,960
Lisa Su, who runs AMD.

273
00:10:36,960 --> 00:10:38,800
Isn't AMD the rival?

274
00:10:38,800 --> 00:10:40,800
Yes, she's also his cousin.

275
00:10:40,800 --> 00:10:43,000
The real list doesn't exist yet.

276
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:45,520
The board hired an outside firm to write it.

277
00:10:45,520 --> 00:10:46,840
Nobody had one ready.

278
00:10:46,840 --> 00:10:49,440
Whoever gets it also inherits Washington,

279
00:10:49,440 --> 00:10:51,520
where Jensen did the talking himself.

280
00:10:51,520 --> 00:10:55,600
Last year, Trump wanted 20% of Nvidia's China chip sales

281
00:10:55,600 --> 00:10:56,400
for the government.

282
00:10:56,400 --> 00:10:58,480
Jensen talked him down to 15.

283
00:10:58,480 --> 00:11:00,800
So we negotiated a little deal.

284
00:11:00,800 --> 00:11:02,400
That deal was personal.

285
00:11:02,400 --> 00:11:04,400
Now, Congress wants answers.

286
00:11:04,400 --> 00:11:06,920
An Nvidia's forecast counts on nothing

287
00:11:06,920 --> 00:11:08,480
from China's data centers.

288
00:11:08,480 --> 00:11:11,640
A new boss inherits the hearings, not the friendship.

289
00:11:11,640 --> 00:11:13,560
Then the president joined the search.

290
00:11:13,560 --> 00:11:15,840
There is only one man to take his place.

291
00:11:15,840 --> 00:11:17,560
Tim Cook of Apple.

292
00:11:17,560 --> 00:11:19,640
A legend replaces a legend.

293
00:11:19,640 --> 00:11:21,920
Doesn't Tim Cook already have a job?

294
00:11:21,920 --> 00:11:25,480
He stepped down as Apple's CEO on September 1st.

295
00:11:25,480 --> 00:11:28,720
So the pick to replace a CEO who just left

296
00:11:28,720 --> 00:11:30,680
is a CEO who just left.

297
00:11:30,680 --> 00:11:34,800
Elon Musk had already said hiring Jensen would be an honor.

298
00:11:34,800 --> 00:11:36,640
Cook is a great operator.

299
00:11:36,640 --> 00:11:39,520
Under him, Apple got the watch and AirPods,

300
00:11:39,520 --> 00:11:41,120
but never another iPhone.

301
00:11:41,120 --> 00:11:43,360
An operator keeps a machine running.

302
00:11:43,360 --> 00:11:46,720
Nvidia's machine was one man making bets.

303
00:11:48,960 --> 00:11:51,200
On Tuesday, Nvidia reports.

304
00:11:51,200 --> 00:11:52,600
What are they expecting?

305
00:11:52,600 --> 00:11:56,000
About $108 billion of sales for the quarter.

306
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:57,440
More than a billion a day.

307
00:11:57,440 --> 00:11:59,240
The numbers will be great.

308
00:11:59,240 --> 00:12:00,360
That's the trap.

309
00:12:00,360 --> 00:12:03,200
Those chips were designed, ordered, and promised

310
00:12:03,200 --> 00:12:04,760
while Jensen was in charge.

311
00:12:04,760 --> 00:12:06,960
The first report after a founder leaves

312
00:12:06,960 --> 00:12:09,360
is the founder's last bets paying out.

313
00:12:09,360 --> 00:12:12,960
Intel kept making money for years while it lost its lead.

314
00:12:12,960 --> 00:12:15,080
Wall Street is split three ways.

315
00:12:15,080 --> 00:12:18,080
Analyst Jay Goldberg says the quarter will be good

316
00:12:18,080 --> 00:12:19,280
and nobody will care.

317
00:12:19,280 --> 00:12:22,200
Investor Dan Niles bet his interviewer dinner

318
00:12:22,200 --> 00:12:25,720
that a strong report sends the stock up, whoever is CEO.

319
00:12:25,720 --> 00:12:28,840
And Ives says it's worth a quarter less without him.

320
00:12:28,840 --> 00:12:30,680
The bulls have a real case.

321
00:12:30,680 --> 00:12:32,800
Nvidia's next three chip generations

322
00:12:32,800 --> 00:12:35,640
are already mapped out through 2028.

323
00:12:35,640 --> 00:12:37,640
In March, Jensen said he could see

324
00:12:37,640 --> 00:12:39,960
at least a trillion dollars of sales

325
00:12:39,960 --> 00:12:42,560
from his two newest chips through next year.

326
00:12:42,560 --> 00:12:45,760
It has about 40,000 employees, not one.

327
00:12:45,760 --> 00:12:47,960
Fund manager Jeremiah Buckley says,

328
00:12:47,960 --> 00:12:51,800
we think visibility is extremely high for 2027

329
00:12:51,800 --> 00:12:55,160
and in 2011, the year jobs stepped down and died,

330
00:12:55,160 --> 00:12:56,560
Apple's stock went up.

331
00:12:56,560 --> 00:12:59,240
So it's fine for two years

332
00:12:59,240 --> 00:13:01,400
when somebody has to make the next bet.

333
00:13:01,400 --> 00:13:03,800
Okay, Tuesday, what am I watching?

334
00:13:03,800 --> 00:13:05,080
Who answers the questions?

335
00:13:05,080 --> 00:13:07,800
For the first time, it won't be Jensen.

336
00:13:07,800 --> 00:13:08,640
And?

337
00:13:08,640 --> 00:13:11,200
Anything after 2028 where the map ends

338
00:13:11,200 --> 00:13:14,200
and China, now that the man who cut the deals is gone.

339
00:13:14,200 --> 00:13:15,920
That's how Nvidia fails.

340
00:13:15,920 --> 00:13:17,680
Not with a crash on Tuesday,

341
00:13:17,680 --> 00:13:19,760
it keeps making money for years

342
00:13:19,760 --> 00:13:21,480
and never makes the next CUDA

343
00:13:21,480 --> 00:13:24,840
because nobody left in charge is paid to bet like that.

344
00:13:24,840 --> 00:13:28,960
In 2013, Morris Chang, the founder of TSMC,

345
00:13:28,960 --> 00:13:30,920
the company that makes Nvidia's chips,

346
00:13:30,920 --> 00:13:33,000
offered Jensen the top job there.

347
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:35,480
Chang spent 10 minutes making his pitch.

348
00:13:35,480 --> 00:13:38,400
Jensen listened and said, no, he already had a job.

349
00:13:38,400 --> 00:13:39,560
He doesn't anymore.

350
00:13:39,560 --> 00:13:42,720
His plan for this day was to pass on everything he knew

351
00:13:42,720 --> 00:13:45,480
as often as he could to everyone at Nvidia.

352
00:13:45,480 --> 00:13:47,160
That two trillion dollar gap

353
00:13:47,160 --> 00:13:49,120
is Wall Street betting that it didn't.

354
00:13:49,120 --> 00:13:52,080
Tuesday won't settle that, the next big bet will.

355
00:13:52,080 --> 00:13:55,160
And right now, nobody knows who gets to make it.

356
